<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="anthocyanidin modification (Arabidopsis)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7450" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The phenylpropanoid biosynthesis (see |FRAME:PWY1F-467|, |FRAME:PWY-361|) generates a multitude of compounds involved in structural integrity, plant defense and interactions with the biotic and abiotic environment |CITS:[20035037]|. It is also the gateway to a number of phenylpropanoid derivatives, for instance flavonoids which fulfill important roles in the reproduction and survival of plants. The model plant &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; represents the most thoroughly researched plant in terms of elucidating the biosynthetic routes, enzymes, encoding genes and regulatory aspects of the diverse compound classes originating from the basic phenylpropanoids |CITS:[Fraser11] [Saito13]|. Descendants of this compounds, e.g. simple anthocyanins are often further modified by sequential hydroxylation, methylation, glucosylation and acylation which often results in complex anthocyanin structures such as the main anthocyanin found in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt;, i.e. |FRAME:CPD-16540| |CITS:[11830144]|. Such modifications are discussed as metabolic events which may alter light-absorption properties, stabilize pigments, effect water solubility, lower enzymatic degradation and enhance their bioavailability in diet |CITS:[Tanaka06] [Strack94]|. The pathway depicted above describes the biosynthetic route of this compound also referred to as A11 according to the classification of |CITS:[15807784]|. Although the various steps leading to A11 have been enzymatically characterized and the reaction sequence is supported by experimental data certain modifications may still occur in a different order, for instance the xylosylation |CITS:[21899608]| or early acylation |CITS:[17425720]| of |FRAME:CPD1F-766| which is illustrated by the fork in the pathway which eventually merges into the formation of |FRAME:CPD-16536|. Nevertheless, in general the biosynthetic route to A11 is thought to proceed in a defined order rather than in a network of reactions |CITS:[17425720]|. Oftentimes enzymes catalyzing modification steps in secondary metabolism are characterized by broad substrate specificity and may not be identifiable through sequence homology which requires variegated approaches including transcriptomics and metabolomics analyses |CITS:[17425720] [15807784] [17292360] [15908604]|. The xylosylation at C-2&quot; and the coumaryolation at C-6&quot; appear to be the first modifications of |FRAME:CPD1F-766| because |FRAME:CPD-7138| is not a suitable acceptor for the corresponding cyanidin 2&quot;-xylosyltransferase |CITS:[21899608]| and cyanidin 6&quot;-coumaroyltransferase |CITS:[17425720]|. Following the 5-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylation of |FRAME:CPD-16536| |CITS:[15807784]| malonysation of the 5-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;glucose moiety is next because the cyanidin 5-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucoside 6&quot;-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-malonyltransferase (5MaT) does not accept more advanced modified cyanide where the coumaroyl group has been further glucosylated |CITS:[17292360]|. &lt;i&gt;In vivo&lt;/i&gt; experiments with &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; mutants with a knocked out serine carboxypeptidase-like (SCPL) enzyme that catalyzes sinapoylation indicate that this is the second to last step in the A11 formation |CITS:[17600138]| which biosynthesis is finalized by an acylglucose dependent anthocyanin glucosyltransferase |CITS:[23298714]|.</Description>
